{"judgeName":"articleQuality:body","judge":"Article Body Quality","judgeModel":"deepseek:deepseek-v4-pro","criteria":["Contains a real, meaningful article body (not an error, fallback notice or junk).","Scores 4-5 pass; 1-3 fail."],"score":5,"maxScore":5,"passingScore":4,"passed":true,"prompt":"Title: Gemini 4 Argon: Built to solve your hardest problems\nSource: GOOGLE AI BLOG\n\nIn this article\n\nSolving complex engineering tasks\nTransforming critical business and professional workflows\nAccelerating real-world impact with researchers and domain experts\nDeveloping Argon responsibly and safely\nGet started today\nGoogle AI Blog\nGemini 4 Argon: Built to solve your hardest problems\nOctober 13, 2026\n11 min read\n\nGemini 4 Argon is now globally available today for developers and enterprises via the API in AI Studio and Gemini platform, for Google AI Ultra subscribers in Google Antigravity, and Google AI Ultra and Pro subscribers in the Gemini app and AI Mode in Search.\n\nArgon excels at deep work in complex, real-world tasks across domains including coding, finance, legal, cybersecurity, and science. Its depth and precision across multi-step tasks leads Argon to outperform other frontier models on benchmarks across domains, ranking #1 on the Vals Index, which measures model performance across critical economic sectors like finance, coding, legal, and tax work.\n\nThe model launches today at an introductory price 1 of $2 per million input tokens and $10 per million output tokens (with cached input tokens at 95% off).\n\nTeams at Google, across enterprises, and in the research community have already been using Argon to land significant impact across fields:\n\nTo solve major engineering problems like large codebase refactoring and optimizations\nTo optimize business workflows across domains like legal, finance, and science\nTo find and patch critical cybersecurity vulnerabilities\nTo enable novel breakthroughs in quantum computing, nuclear fusion, and life sciences\nSolving complex engineering tasks\n\nAt Google, we’ve been using Argon on our hardest engineering problems. Over the past few weeks, software engineers have been shipping high-quality code faster than ever. Argon in Antigravity has helped teams increase agentic code submissions by more than 35% while reducing code rollbacks by nearly a third.\n\nUntangling browser feature code in Chrome: Argon helped the Chrome team separate core webpage handling from a ~6 million line legacy folder of browser features held within Chromium’s 40 million-line codebase. Piping multiple clean code changes at a time, Argon helped the team finish an architectural refactor across hundreds of files, completing months of work in weeks. For Chrome's billions of global users, this will unlock the ability for live webpages to move fluidly between tabs, side panels, and floating windows without reloading, eliminating an entire category of browser crashes.\nOptimizing a production Python codebase in hours: Argon in Antigravity is helping the team behind Google's open-source Agent Development Kit (ADK), a framework powering enterprise agents, push their performance engineering further. The team gave Argon their .agents/skills rulebook and a one-paragraph goal to eliminate framework overhead without breaking existing apps. Working autonomously overnight, Argon surfaced optimizations, now live in google/adk-python, that cut in-memory session prep time by 40% in long sessions and sped up cold imports by 21%. Read the engineering deep dive to learn more.\nQuantum chemistry open-source package optimization: xTB (Semiempirical Extended Tight-Binding) is one of the most-cited open-source quantum chemistry packages for molecules. Scientists use it to predict molecules' 3D shapes and to screen large numbers of drug and materials candidates. Using Argon in Antigravity, our researchers made xTB up to 2.3x faster at predicting molecular structures and 1.8x faster at conformer search. The changes have been contributed to the official xTB repository on GitHub.\nMaking our open Gemma models nearly three times as fast: Using Argon, we achieved a 2.85x end-to-end inference speedup on an A100 via vLLM (accelerating throughput from ~37,900 to over 108,000 tokens per second on Gemma 4 E2B) with 100% token parity and zero quality drop in just a few days, a process that would usually take months. Check out the upstream pu …[truncated]","response":"In this article\n\nSolving complex engineering tasks\nTransforming critical business and professional workflows\nAccelerating real-world impact with researchers and domain experts\nDeveloping Argon responsibly and safely\nGet started today\nGoogle AI Blog\nGemini 4 Argon: Built to solve your hardest problems\nOctober 13, 2026\n11 min read\n\nGemini 4 Argon is now globally available today for developers and enterprises via the API in AI Studio and Gemini platform, for Google AI Ultra subscribers in Google Antigravity, and Google AI Ultra and Pro subscribers in the Gemini app and AI Mode in Search.\n\nArgon excels at deep work in complex, real-world tasks across domains including coding, finance, legal, cybersecurity, and science. Its depth and precision across multi-step tasks leads Argon to outperform other frontier models on benchmarks across domains, ranking #1 on the Vals Index, which measures model performance across critical economic sectors like finance, coding, legal, and tax work.\n\nThe model launches today at an introductory price 1 of $2 per million input tokens and $10 per million output tokens (with cached input tokens at 95% off).\n\nTeams at Google, across enterprises, and in the research community have already been using Argon to land significant impact across fields:\n\nTo solve major engineering problems like large codebase refactoring and optimizations\nTo optimize business workflows across domains like legal, finance, and science\nTo find and patch critical cybersecurity vulnerabilities\nTo enable novel breakthroughs in quantum computing, nuclear fusion, and life sciences\nSolving complex engineering tasks\n\nAt Google, we’ve been using Argon on our hardest engineering problems. Over the past few weeks, software engineers have been shipping high-quality code faster than ever. Argon in Antigravity has helped teams increase agentic code submissions by more than 35% while reducing code rollbacks by nearly a third.\n\nUntangling browser feature code in Chrome: Argon helped the Chrome team separate core webpage handling from a ~6 million line legacy folder of browser features held within Chromium’s 40 million-line codebase. Piping multiple clean code changes at a time, Argon helped the team finish an architectural refactor across hundreds of files, completing months of work in weeks. For Chrome's billions of global users, this will unlock the ability for live webpages to move fluidly between tabs, side panels, and floating windows without reloading, eliminating an entire category of browser crashes.\nOptimizing a production Python codebase in hours: Argon in Antigravity is helping the team behind Google's open-source Agent Development Kit (ADK), a framework powering enterprise agents, push their performance engineering further. The team gave Argon their .agents/skills rulebook and a one-paragraph goal to eliminate framework overhead without breaking existing apps. Working autonomously overnight, Argon surfaced optimizations, now live in google/adk-python, that cut in-memory session prep time by 40% in long sessions and sped up cold imports by 21%. Read the engineering deep dive to learn more.\nQuantum chemistry open-source package optimization: xTB (Semiempirical Extended Tight-Binding) is one of the most-cited open-source quantum chemistry packages for molecules. Scientists use it to predict molecules' 3D shapes and to screen large numbers of drug and materials candidates. Using Argon in Antigravity, our researchers made xTB up to 2.3x faster at predicting molecular structures and 1.8x faster at conformer search. The changes have been contributed to the official xTB repository on GitHub.\nMaking our open Gemma models nearly three times as fast: Using Argon, we achieved a 2.85x end-to-end inference speedup on an A100 via vLLM (accelerating throughput from ~37,900 to over 108,000 tokens per second on Gemma 4 E2B) with 100% token parity and zero quality drop in just a few days, a process that would usually take months. Check out the upstream pu …[truncated]","reasoning":"The modal contains a full article body with multiple coherent paragraphs, sections, and specific examples about Gemini 4 Argon, indicating substantive content despite a truncation at the end.","titlePath":["Content quality @upstream","Live Feed: every probed card renders a real article in the reader"],"testFile":"tests/contentQuality.spec.ts","retry":0,"timestamp":"2026-10-17T11:45:54.137Z"}
{"judgeName":"articleQuality:body","judge":"Article Body Quality","judgeModel":"deepseek:deepseek-v4-pro","criteria":["Contains a real, meaningful article body (not an error, fallback notice or junk).","Scores 4-5 pass; 1-3 fail."],"score":5,"maxScore":5,"passingScore":4,"passed":true,"prompt":"Title: Agentic AI Test Automation Guide | mabl\nSource: MABL BLOG\n\nIn this article\n\nKey Takeaways\nWhy Agentic AI Test Automation Is Emerging Now\nWhat Makes Test Automation Agentic?\nHow Agentic AI Test Automation Differs From Traditional and AI-Assisted Testing\nThe Agentic Testing Loop: From Goals to Quality Signals\nWhat Can Teams Use Agentic AI Test Automation For?\nWhat Should Teams Look for in an Agentic AI Test Automation Platform?\nHow mabl Supports Agentic AI Test Automation\nMabl Blog\nUnderstanding Agentic AI Test Automation: A Practical Guide for Software Teams\nAbbey Charles\nOctober 13, 2026\n9 min read\nKey Takeaways\nAgentic AI test automation helps teams move from isolated task automation to testing workflows that support planning, execution, adaptation, analysis, and human review.\nThe strongest agentic workflows help teams manage coverage, maintenance, visibility, and release confidence as applications change.\nAI-assisted test creation can help teams move faster, but enterprise testing still needs independent verification, governance, and clear quality signals.\nTeams should evaluate agentic AI testing tools by context, control, lifecycle coverage, workflow fit, and support for human oversight.\n\nAI-assisted development can increase output faster than many quality workflows can validate it. Teams can generate code, create test drafts, and fix issues quickly. They still need reliable coverage, maintainable tests, clear visibility, and independent verification of outputs from faster workflows.\n\nThat is where agentic AI test automation comes in. Instead of only helping with a single task, agentic testing workflows can help teams plan, run, adapt, analyze, and maintain tests under human oversight.\n\nStill, faster test creation only solves part of the problem. Teams also need an agentic testing platform that supports reliable quality signals, governance, and workflows that fit how they already build and release software.\n\nWhy Agentic AI Test Automation Is Emerging Now\n\nSoftware delivery is moving faster, but quality validation still has to answer hard questions, like:\n\nDid the change break a critical workflow?\nDoes the application still behave as expected across web, mobile, and APIs?\nCan the team trust the signal before release?\n\nTraditional automation helps, but it depends on people to create, update, debug, and interpret tests as the application changes.\n\nAI-assisted tools can reduce some of that work by drafting tests or summarizing failures. But faster test creation is only one part of the problem.\n\nMore code and faster releases create more validation work, especially when teams are already dealing with brittle suites, manual bottlenecks, and fragmented tools.\n\nAgentic AI test automation isn't a completely new concept, but it has been gaining more attention recently, largely due to demand. Teams require systems that can support a greater portion of the testing lifecycle. Additionally, this technology has evolved significantly in recent years.\n\nThe goal is not “hands-off autonomy.” It is a testing workflow that can help plan around quality goals, adapt as the product changes, surface risk, and keep people in control of release decisions. Teams need quality signals across the full user journey, not only faster checks near the code.\n\nOur Introducing Active Coverage guide expands on this shift: as development accelerates, coverage must keep pace without burdening QA and engineering teams with additional maintenance.\n\nWhat Makes Test Automation Agentic?\n\nAgentic test automation uses AI to support more of the testing workflow, from deciding what needs to be covered to analyzing failures and