h a single task, agentic testing workflows can help teams plan, run, adapt, analyze, and maintain tests under human oversight.\n\nStill, faster test creation only solves part of the problem. Teams also need an agentic testing platform that supports reliable quality signals, governance, and workflows that fit how they already build and release software.\n\nWhy Agentic AI Test Automation Is Emerging Now\n\nSoftware delivery is moving faster, but quality validation still has to answer hard questions, like:\n\nDid the change break a critical workflow?\nDoes the application still behave as expected across web, mobile, and APIs?\nCan the team trust the signal before release?\n\nTraditional automation helps, but it depends on people to create, update, debug, and interpret tests as the application changes.\n\nAI-assisted tools can reduce some of that work by drafting tests or summarizing failures. But faster test creation is only one part of the problem.\n\nMore code and faster releases create more validation work, especially when teams are already dealing with brittle suites, manual bottlenecks, and fragmented tools.\n\nAgentic AI test automation isn't a completely new concept, but it has been gaining more attention recently, largely due to demand. Teams require systems that can support a greater portion of the testing lifecycle. Additionally, this technology has evolved significantly in recent years.\n\nThe goal is not “hands-off autonomy.” It is a testing workflow that can help plan around quality goals, adapt as the product changes, surface risk, and keep people in control of release decisions. Teams need quality signals across the full user journey, not only faster checks near the code.\n\nOur Introducing Active Coverage guide expands on this shift: as development accelerates, coverage must keep pace without burdening QA and engineering teams with additional maintenance.\n\nWhat Makes Test Automation Agentic?\n\nAgentic test automation uses AI to support more of the testing workflow, from deciding what needs to be covered to analyzing failures and helping teams adapt tests as applications change.\n\nThe agentic part comes from how the system behaves. It can work by setting goals, taking action, observing results, reasoning through changes, and sharing quality signals with the people responsible for release decisions.\n\nHuman oversight stays central. Teams can define quality goals, decide which risks matter most, review recommendations, and guide the testin …[truncated]","reasoning":"The text contains a substantive, well-structured article with multiple coherent paragraphs, headings, and a table discussing agentic AI test automation, indicating real, meaningful content.","titlePath":["Content quality @upstream","Live Feed: every probed card renders a real article in the reader"],"testFile":"tests/contentQuality.spec.ts","retry":0,"timestamp":"2026-10-17T11:45:58.289Z"}
{"judgeName":"articleQuality:body","judge":"Article Body Quality","judgeModel":"deepseek:deepseek-v4-pro","criteria":["Contains a real, meaningful article body (not an error, fallback notice or junk).","Scores 4-5 pass; 1-3 fail."],"score":5,"maxScore":5,"passingScore":4,"passed":true,"prompt":"Title: Find elements the way your users do with cy.getByRole()\nSource: CYPRESS BLOG\n\nIn this article\n\nWhat cy.getByRole() does\nWhy native elements are the default in cy.getByRole()\nMoving off of Cypress Testing Library\nHow cy.getByRole() and Cypress Accessibility fit together\nWhat to do next\nFrequently asked questions\nCypress Blog\nFind elements the way your users do with cy.getByRole()\nOctober 14, 2026\n11 min read\n\nStarting in Cypress 16.2.0, cy.getByRole() is a built-in query that finds elements by their role and accessible name. The role is what a screen reader announces an element as, such as a button or a link, and the accessible name is the text it announces for it.\n\nThis offers a built-in alternative to the .findByRole() selector from the popular Cypress Testing Library plugin. That package no longer has an active maintainer and the previous maintainer suggested Cypress build these queries into Cypress directly.\n\nWe agree with that direction. Role-based queries are a core way to write resilient, accessible tests, and they belong in Cypress itself. cy.getByRole() is the first of these built-in queries. We plan to bring more of the Testing Library queries teams depend on into Cypress.\n\nWhat cy.getByRole() does\n\ncy.getByRole() finds every element with a given role, such as button, link, heading, or tab, and can narrow the results to elements whose accessible name matches. Because those are what assistive technology announces, a test written this way finds elements the way assistive technology does. If a control’s role or label changes, the test fails.\n\ndescribe('account settings', () => {\n  it('saves profile changes', () => {\n    cy.visit('/settings')\n    cy.getByRole('textbox', { name: 'Display name' }).type('Jane Doe')\n    cy.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save changes' }).click()\n    cy.getByRole('alert').should('have.text', 'Changes saved')\n  })\n})\n\nA few behaviors on how cy.getByRole() works:\n\nIt yields every match. Like cy.get(), cy.getByRole() yields all matching elements in document order. Narrow the name, or chain .first() or .eq(), to act on one.\nA string name matches the whole name, case-sensitively. Pass a regular expression to match part of a name or ignore case, or a function for custom logic.\nIt skips elements hidden from the accessibility tree by default, such as elements with aria-hidden=\"true\" or display: none. Pass hidden: true to include them.\nIt retries like any Cypress query, so it waits for an element to appear and for chained assertions to pass.\nIt prefers built-in HTML elements. If a native HTML element exists for a given role, cy.getByRole() matches that element only, unless native: false is passed.\n\nTo narrow by state, such as checked, selected, expanded, or heading level, chain .filter():\n\n// The tab that is currently selected\ncy.getByRole('tab').filter('[aria-selected=\"true\"]')\n\n// Level 2 headings\ncy.getByRole('heading').filter('[aria-level=\"2\"], h2:not([aria-level])')\n\n// A checked native checkbox\ncy.getByRole('checkbox').filter(':checked')\n\n// A checked checkbox that gets its role from a role attribute\ncy.getByRole('checkbox', { native: false }).filter('[aria-checked=\"true\"]')\n\n// The menu button whose menu is open\ncy.getByRole('button').filter('[aria-expanded=\"true\"]')\n\n// A toggle button that is switched on\ncy.getByRole('button').filter('[aria-pressed=\"true\"]')\n\n// The link to the page the reader is on\ncy.getByRole('link').filter('[aria-current=\"page\"]')\n\n// Every button except disabled ones\ncy.getByRole('button').not(':disabled, [aria-disabled=\"true\"]')\n\nThe cy.getByRole() API reference covers every option, including shadow DOM, TypeScript, and scoping with .within().\n\nWhy native elements are the default in cy.getByRole()\n\nFor any role that HTML has a native element for, such as button, link, heading, dialog, or checkbox, cy.getByRole() matches only elements whose tag gives them that role. A <div role=\"button\"> does not match cy.getByRole('button') unless you pass native: false.\n\n<button>Save</button>\n<div role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\">Cancel</div>\n// Yields and clicks the nati …[truncated]","response":"In this article\n\nWhat cy.getByRole() does\nWhy native elements are the default in cy.getByRole()\nMoving off of Cypress Testing Library\nHow cy.getByRole() and Cypress Accessibility fit together\nWhat to do next\nFrequently asked questions\nCypress Blog\nFind elements the way your users do with cy.getByRole()\nOctober 14, 2026\n11 min read\n\nStarting in Cypress 16.2.0, cy.getByRole() is a built-in query that finds elements by their role and accessible name. The role is what a screen reader announces an element as, such as a button or a link, and the accessible name is the text it announces for it.\n\nThis offers a built-in alternative to the .findByRole() selector from the popular Cypress Testing Library plugin. That package no longer has an active maintainer and the previous maintainer suggested Cypress build these queries into Cypress directly.\n\nWe agree with that direction. Role-based queries are a core way to write resilient, accessible tests, and they belong in Cypress itself. cy.getByRole() is the first of these built-in queries. We plan to bring more of the Testing Library queries teams depend on into Cypress.\n\nWhat cy.getByRole() does\n\ncy.getByRole() finds every element with a given role, such as button, link, heading, or tab, and can narrow the results to elements whose accessible name matches. Because those are what assistive technology announces, a test written this way finds elements the way assistive technology does. If a control’s role or label c